import{_ as t,o as a,c as i,ao as r}from"./chunks/framework.D26l3Sq6.js";const g=JSON.parse('{"title":"The WrapIt driver","description":"","frontmatter":{},"headers":[],"relativePath":"implementation/wrapit.md","filePath":"implementation/wrapit.md","lastUpdated":null}'),s={name:"implementation/wrapit.md"};function o(n,e,d,l,c,h){return a(),i("div",null,[...e[0]||(e[0]=[r(`<h1 id="The-WrapIt-driver" tabindex="-1">The WrapIt driver <a class="header-anchor" href="#The-WrapIt-driver" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;The WrapIt driver {#The-WrapIt-driver}&quot;">​</a></h1><p><code>gen/wrapit/</code> contains the pipeline that generates the C++ binding module from VTK headers using <a href="https://github.com/grasph/wrapit" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">WrapIt</a> (libclang -&gt; CxxWrap code), replacing hand-writing of the bulk method surface. <strong>This is the generator behind the shipped library</strong>: <code>gen/build_wrapper.jl</code> drives it and installs the merged library at <code>gen/wrapit/build/shipped/libvtk_julia.so</code>.</p><p>The approach follows the same pattern as <a href="https://github.com/JuliaHEP/Geant4.jl" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Geant4.jl</a> and <a href="https://github.com/JuliaHEP/ROOT.jl" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">ROOT.jl</a>: WrapIt generates the C++ bindings from the library headers, a companion shim layer handles ownership and convenience methods, and the result is distributed as a JLL binary package <a href="https://github.com/JuliaBinaryWrappers/VTK_julia_jll.jl" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">here</a>.</p><h2 id="Quick-start" tabindex="-1">Quick start <a class="header-anchor" href="#Quick-start" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Quick start {#Quick-start}&quot;">​</a></h2><div class="language-shell vp-adaptive-theme"><button title="Copy Code" class="copy"></button><span class="lang">shell</span><pre class="shiki shiki-themes github-light github-dark vp-code" tabindex="0"><code><span class="line"><span style="--shiki-light:#6F42C1;--shiki-dark:#B392F0;">julia</span><span style="--shiki-light:#005CC5;--shiki-dark:#79B8FF;"> --project=.</span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;"> gen/build_wrapper.jl</span><span style="--shiki-light:#6A737D;--shiki-dark:#6A737D;">        # generate + merge + compile + install</span></span>
<span class="line"><span style="--shiki-light:#6F42C1;--shiki-dark:#B392F0;">julia</span><span style="--shiki-light:#005CC5;--shiki-dark:#79B8FF;"> --project=gen/wrapit</span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;"> gen/wrapit/build.jl</span><span style="--shiki-light:#6A737D;--shiki-dark:#6A737D;">    # same, without the install step</span></span>
<span class="line"><span style="--shiki-light:#6F42C1;--shiki-dark:#B392F0;">julia</span><span style="--shiki-light:#005CC5;--shiki-dark:#79B8FF;"> --project=gen/wrapit</span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;"> gen/wrapit/generated_library_integration.jl</span><span style="--shiki-light:#6A737D;--shiki-dark:#6A737D;">  # analytic-oracle gate</span></span></code></pre></div><p><code>build.jl</code> renders the WrapIt config with runtime artifact paths, <strong>builds <a href="https://github.com/grasph/wrapit" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">upstream WrapIt</a> from source</strong> (<code>wrapit_src.jl</code>), runs it, generates the mechanical shim (<code>auto_shim.jl</code>), and produces smart-pointer forwarding overloads (<code>smartptr_wrappers.jl</code> -&gt; <code>src/generated_methods.jl</code>), post-processes the generated C++, and compiles <strong>one merged library</strong> (<code>gen/wrapit/build/shipped/libvtk_julia.so</code>, gitignored) from:</p><ol><li><p>the WrapIt-generated code (~60 classes, 1200+ methods, true VTK inheritance),</p></li><li><p>an <strong>auto-generated mechanical shim</strong> (<code>auto_shim.jl</code> → <code>build/cxx/shim_auto.cpp</code>, ~25 methods): owning <code>X_new</code> factories (detected from <code>static vtkX* New();</code>) and gap-filling/string accessors derived from the VTK accessor macros,</p></li><li><p>a hand shim (<code>shim_vtkjl.{h,cpp}</code>, ~450 lines) merged into the same <code>JLCXX_MODULE</code> — smart-pointer traits, ownership policy (<code>take_ref</code>), the error window, zero-copy workers, and <strong>one generic <code>SetInputData</code>/<code>GetOutput</code> for all algorithms</strong> (via <code>vtkAlgorithm::SetInputDataObject</code>/<code>GetOutputDataObject</code>, with downcasts in the Julian layer).</p></li></ol><p>The source defaults to upstream <code>main</code>. Set <code>VTKJL_WRAPIT_UPDATE=1</code> to advance an existing checkout, or use <code>VTKJL_WRAPIT_REPO</code> and <code>VTKJL_WRAPIT_REF</code> to select another repository, a release tag, or a pinned full commit SHA (release builds use <code>gen/wrapit/wrapit.pin</code>; a SHA is fetched directly since <code>git clone -b</code> only accepts branches/tags). Changing the configured repository or ref also triggers a rebuild.</p><p>The auto-shim skips methods the hand shim already provides (dedup by class+name) and methods WrapIt already generated (normalized-signature match), so there are no duplicate registrations; string getters intentionally override the generated <code>const char*</code> versions with <code>std::string</code> ones via the clash audit.</p><h2 id="The-companion-layer" tabindex="-1">The companion layer <a class="header-anchor" href="#The-companion-layer" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;The companion layer {#The-companion-layer}&quot;">​</a></h2><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th style="text-align:right;">piece</th><th style="text-align:right;">why</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td style="text-align:right;"><code>VTK.wit.in</code></td><td style="text-align:right;">input headers — <strong>every link of every inheritance chain</strong> must be listed or Julia-side upcasts break (<code>MethodError</code> on inherited calls)</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:right;"><code>veto.txt</code></td><td style="text-align:right;">small hand-maintained list: ostream wrappers and the getters whose ownership the shim overrides. The macro-accessor vetoes are <strong>gone</strong> (upstream WrapIt no longer truncates macro-generated methods); clash vetoes are still re-derived mechanically by <code>veto_shim_clashes!()</code> when needed</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:right;">post-processor in <code>build.jl</code></td><td style="text-align:right;">AOS leaf arrays and other public classes whose direct parent is unmapped are re-pointed at their nearest registered VTK ancestor with <code>jlcxx::SuperType</code>. This covers structured data, multiblock datasets/readers/writers, and <code>vtkIdTypeArray</code>. Also strips WrapIt&#39;s parametric <code>vtkSmartPointer</code> wrapper (the shim registers the real one) and injects the shim into the module init</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:right;"><code>veto_shim_clashes!()</code> / <code>check_shim_types_registered!()</code> in <code>build.jl</code></td><td style="text-align:right;">mechanical audits, re-run on every build: generated methods colliding with the shim are vetoed (duplicate Julia methods are fatal at precompile), and every vtk type the shim references must be registered by the generated code</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:right;"><code>shim_vtkjl.{h,cpp}</code></td><td style="text-align:right;">everything that needs a human decision: smart-pointer traits + <code>add_smart_pointer</code>, <code>take_ref</code>&#39;d getters/outputs, the error window, zero-copy views, <strong>generic <code>SetInputData</code>/<code>GetOutput</code>/<code>Update</code> on <code>vtkAlgorithm</code></strong> (no per-filter plumbing), downcasts, explicit AOS-leaf up-casts (<code>upcast_dataarray</code>)</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:right;"><code>auto_shim.jl</code></td><td style="text-align:right;">generates the mechanical shim from the headers: <code>X_new</code> factories and macro accessors. Only public members, non-template classes, whitelisted scalar types; dedups against the hand shim and against WrapIt&#39;s own output</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:right;"><code>smartptr_wrappers.jl</code></td><td style="text-align:right;">generates <code>src/generated_methods.jl</code> from actual raw-pointer registrations. These Julia overloads accept smart pointers and forward through <code>_cp</code>, eliminating explicit conversions at ordinary call sites while retaining the low-level raw-pointer API</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:right;"><code>generated_library_integration.jl</code></td><td style="text-align:right;">Low-level integration test: loads the merged library directly (<code>@wrapmodule</code>, no package needed) and replays the unit-hex → clip (tetrahedron oracle) → write/read <code>.vtu</code> pipeline</td></tr></tbody></table><p>Ownership of factory/getter returns stays in the shim layer (<code>take_ref</code> + smart-pointer traits).</p><h2 id="Extending-the-wrapped-surface" tabindex="-1">Extending the wrapped surface <a class="header-anchor" href="#Extending-the-wrapped-surface" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Extending the wrapped surface {#Extending-the-wrapped-surface}&quot;">​</a></h2><ol><li><p>Add the class&#39;s header (and any missing intermediate base-class headers) to <code>input</code> in <code>VTK.wit.in</code>.</p></li><li><p>Run <code>build.jl</code>; the generator builds upstream WrapIt from source on first use (git + CMake + libclang + OpenSSL headers needed). Shim clashes are vetoed mechanically (&quot;added N shim-clash vetoes; REGENERATE&quot; —&gt; just run it again). <strong>Factories and macro accessors come for free; the generic <code>SetInputData</code>/<code>GetOutput</code> covers every algorithm</strong>, so most new filters/readers/writers need no shim edits at all.</p></li><li><p>Only if you need ownership policy, a downcast, or a convenience the generator cannot infer, add it to <code>shim_vtkjl.cpp</code>.</p></li><li><p>Verify with <code>generated_library_integration.jl</code>, then with the main test suite.</p></li></ol><h2 id="Publishing-a-wrapper-release" tabindex="-1">Publishing a wrapper release <a class="header-anchor" href="#Publishing-a-wrapper-release" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Publishing a wrapper release {#Publishing-a-wrapper-release}&quot;">​</a></h2><p>Development on <code>main</code> never touches the published wrapper. To release:</p><ol><li><p>On <code>main</code>, commit the release prep: bump <code>version</code> in <code>Project.toml</code> to the release version (the tag name, e.g. <code>0.2.0</code>) and <code>[compat] VTK_julia_jll</code> to the wrapper version you are about to publish (declaring compat with a not-yet-registered JLL is fine), and update <code>gen/wrapit/wrapit.pin</code> if advancing WrapIt: <code>git ls-remote https://github.com/grasph/wrapit.git refs/heads/main</code></p></li><li><p>Tag that commit and push only that tag:</p><div class="language-shell vp-adaptive-theme"><button title="Copy Code" class="copy"></button><span class="lang">shell</span><pre class="shiki shiki-themes github-light github-dark vp-code" tabindex="0"><code><span class="line"><span style="--shiki-light:#6F42C1;--shiki-dark:#B392F0;">git</span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;"> tag</span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;"> v0.2.0</span><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;"> &amp;&amp; </span><span style="--shiki-light:#6F42C1;--shiki-dark:#B392F0;">git</span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;"> push</span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;"> origin</span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;"> v0.2.0</span></span></code></pre></div><p>The <code>PublishWrapper</code> workflow (<code>.github/workflows/PublishWrapper.yml</code>) then checks out the tag, exports <code>VTKJL_WRAPIT_REF</code> from <code>wrapit.pin</code> so the same tag always generates the same sources, builds the wrapper, and requires a clean tracked tree afterwards (generation may rewrite <code>src/generated_methods.jl</code> and <code>gen/wrapit/veto.txt</code> — those must be committed in the tag). It gates on <code>generated_library_integration.jl</code> and on the full test suite run against the freshly built library via <code>VTKJL_LIB</code>, then runs <code>gen/publish_wrapper.jl</code>. That script copies exactly the <code>MANIFEST</code>-listed files (generated <code>build/cxx/*</code> + <code>build/jl/VTKGen.jl</code></p><ul><li>the hand shim) into a fresh clone of</li></ul><p><a href="https://github.com/ffrancesco94/VTK_cxxwrap" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">VTK_cxxwrap</a>, records the VTK.jl and WrapIt commit SHAs in the commit message, tags with the same version, and pushes (via the <code>VTK_CXXWRAP_TOKEN</code> secret). It refuses to overwrite an existing remote tag and errors if the generator produced a source file that is not in the manifest. The same script works locally after <code>gen/build_wrapper.jl</code>:</p><div class="language-shell vp-adaptive-theme"><button title="Copy Code" class="copy"></button><span class="lang">shell</span><pre class="shiki shiki-themes github-light github-dark vp-code" tabindex="0"><code><span class="line"><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;">VTKJL_WRAPPER_VERSION</span><span style="--shiki-light:#D73A49;--shiki-dark:#F97583;">=</span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;">0.2.0</span><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;"> VTKJL_CXXWRAP_DIR</span><span style="--shiki-light:#D73A49;--shiki-dark:#F97583;">=</span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;">../packaging/VTK_cxxwrap</span><span style="--shiki-light:#6F42C1;--shiki-dark:#B392F0;"> \\</span></span>
<span class="line"><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;">  julia</span><span style="--shiki-light:#005CC5;--shiki-dark:#79B8FF;"> --project=.</span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;"> gen/publish_wrapper.jl</span></span></code></pre></div></li><li><p>Update Yggdrasil&#39;s <code>V/VTK_julia/build_tarballs.jl</code> (the <code>GitSource</code> commit of VTK_cxxwrap and the <code>version</code>) and open the PR. Once <code>VTK_julia_jll</code> is registered, the VTK.jl tag becomes installable; until then, runs on the tag cannot resolve its declared compat, which is expected.</p></li></ol><p>Users install release tags (<code>Pkg.add(url=..., rev=&quot;v0.2.0&quot;)</code>) to get the matching prebuilt JLL; on <code>main</code> they build the wrapper locally and use <code>VTKJL_LIB</code>.</p><h2 id="Upstream-bugs" tabindex="-1">Upstream bugs <a class="header-anchor" href="#Upstream-bugs" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Upstream bugs {#Upstream-bugs}&quot;">​</a></h2><p>Four WrapIt issues were identified while developing the driver: (1) silent truncation on macro-generated methods; (2) <code>inheritances</code> ignored for template parents; (3) Julia keywords exported; (4) <code>std::basic_ostream</code> wrapped into uncompilable code. <strong>Bug 1 is fixed and validated upstream</strong>: <a href="https://github.com/grasph/wrapit/issues/76" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">issue #76</a> was closed by commit <a href="https://github.com/grasph/wrapit/commit/408e3c1d6b4c012cc7ac7ec51c0a15d791a29822" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer"><code>408e3c1</code></a>, which also added a macro-generation regression test. The driver builds upstream WrapIt, so the ~200 macro-accessor vetoes are gone. Bugs 2–4 remain (workarounds: AOS post-processor, <code>export_blacklist</code>, ostream vetoes in <code>veto.txt</code>).</p>`,20)])])}const u=t(s,[["render",o]]);export{g as __pageData,u as default};
